  "slug": "models-agent-level-3abc",
  "summary": "Recent work pairs agent- and subpopulation-level variation with formal attribution of stochastic noise in disease and population models.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the treatment of stochasticity and between-individual variation in models of disease and structured populations. A global sensitivity analysis separates noise channels in a stochastic yellow fever spillover model, and a conditional tabular GAN emulates road-injury agents within a multi-disease agent-based model, reducing run-time by 86.6% while retaining its dynamics. What is new is pairing agent-level variation with formal attribution of stochastic variability. The remaining two items extend the theme to inverse problems: a frequency-domain route to approximately Gaussian measurement likelihoods for Bayesian identification of reaction networks, and a PINN-based framework that infers growth, renewal and recruitment functions in a size-structured fish oogenesis model from cross-sectional data.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-09"
}
